Scope checking (DIP1000) is always enabled

`@safe` code is now checked for escaping references to stack memory without
any compiler switch. For example, these are errors:

---
int* escapeParam(scope int* p) @safe { return p; }   // scope parameter returned
int* escapeLocal() @safe { int x; return &x; }        // address of a local returned
int* global;
void store(scope int* p) @safe { global = p; }        // scope value stored in a global
---

Taking the address of a local is allowed in `@safe` code as long as the
pointer does not outlive it:

---
void ok() @safe
{
    int x;
    int* p = &x;    // fine: p is inferred `scope`
    *p = 1;
}
---

$(UL
$(LI `-preview=dip1000` and `-dip1000` are still accepted but no longer have
any effect; remove them.)
$(LI `-revert=dip1000` has been removed. Scope checking cannot be turned off.)
$(LI An array literal passed to a `scope` parameter is placed on the stack
instead of the GC heap. When `scope` was only inferred, this is no longer done
for literals of elements with pointers, or outside function bodies (static and
enum initialisers, default arguments); those cases miscompiled.)
$(LI An `opApply` whose delegate parameter is not `scope` makes the `foreach`
body a GC-allocated closure in code that returns from inside the loop. Mark
the delegate parameter `scope`, which is also required in `@nogc` code:
`int opApply(scope int delegate(ref T) dg)`.)
)

Fixed along the way: returning a struct from inside such a `foreach` could
return an unconstructed value or crash (the result was written into the
closure instead of the caller's return slot), and returning from a nested
`foreach` over `opApply` was wrongly rejected with "scope parameter may not
be returned".
